    "body": "So the scenario in discussion is:\r\nA (the opener) opens popup B with the policy denied.\r\nB is currently focused.\r\nB then calls `opener_element.focus()`.\r\n\r\nMy understanding is that this behavior should be allowed, since B is not trying to take focus away from some already‑focused ancestor.\r\n\r\nI put together the table below to capture all possible `focus()` calls in this scenario, assuming B has the policy denied. (If B had the policy allowed, everything would be permitted)\r\n\r\nFocused document | Caller document | Target document | Focus call is allowed\r\n-- | -- | -- | --\r\nA | A | A | Yes\r\nA | A | B | Yes\r\nB | A | A | Yes\r\nB | A | B | Yes\r\nA | B | A | No\r\nA | B | B | No\r\nB | B | A | Yes\r\nB | B | B | Yes\r\n\r\nUnder this model, the only disallowed cases are when B calls `focus()` while A is currently focused, because that represents B trying to move focus away from A without permission.\r\n\r\n@annevk, does this interpretation match what you intended? If this behavior seems reasonable, I can go into the spec details next and update the PR accordingly.",
