    "body": "> I'm likely missing something, but why do we need to use now current global's document. What behavior do we want with this approach vs using target?\r\n \r\n@smaug----, tl;dr is that the idea behind the policy is to prevent frames from stealing focus from their parent, not to restrict what a frame can do once it has focus.\r\n \r\nIn this PR I'm trying to look at the frame that initiated the action (the \"setter\" or \"initiator\" frame) to see if it (or any of its descendants) has focus before moving it somewhere else. My understanding is that `current` global corresponds to the Realm of the initiator frame here (please correct me if I'm wrong).\r\n \r\nFor example, in a simple case where frame A embeds frame B and A has the policy enabled but it's disabled for B, if A is focused and it calls `b_window.focus()` then that should be allowed (by checking if A has focus, not B).\r\n \r\nThis would close the spec gap after [2024 TPAC resolution](https://github.com/w3c/webappsec-permissions-policy/issues/273#issuecomment-2384287101) that \"Focus delegation should also be allowed (allow parent frame programmatically set focus into child iframe)\".\r\n \r\nThere are more concrete scenarios discussed in this issue https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11839.\r\n \r\n> And how does this work with crossSiteWin.focus()? What would current global be when that [focus()](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-window-focus) algorithm runs? (I'm not sure HTML spec is currently super clear on how that all works if the implementation uses separate processes for different sites.)\r\n \r\nI agree the spec doesn't say much about cross origin execution contexts. But as far as I can tell there's no difference or special case for these, so I understand the current global would still be the initiator's Realm and not the target's.",
